MIT_18_703_LECTURE_EXTENSIONS.update({
    12: [{
        "title": "Classification of finitely generated abelian groups",
        "statement": r"Every finitely generated abelian group is isomorphic to \(\mathbb{Z}^r\times\mathbb{Z}/n_1\mathbb{Z}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}/n_k\mathbb{Z}\), where \(n_1\mid n_2\mid\cdots\mid n_k\), and these invariants are unique.",
        "proof": r"Choose generators and express all relations as rows of an integer matrix \(A\). Integer row and column operations correspond to changing relation and generator bases, so they do not change the presented group. The Smith normal form reduces \(A\) to \(\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\ldots,d_k,0,\ldots,0)\) with \(d_i\mid d_{i+1}\). A diagonal relation \(d_i e_i=0\) contributes \(\mathbb{Z}/d_i\mathbb{Z}\), while each zero column contributes a free \(\mathbb{Z}\) factor. Uniqueness follows because determinantal divisors recover the products \(d_1\cdots d_i\).",
        "example_title": "Reading a diagonal presentation",
        "example": r"The presentation with relation matrix \(\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&6\end{pmatrix}\) gives \(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\). A zero third column would add a free \(\mathbb{Z}\) factor.",
    }],
    14: [{
        "title": "Subrings, function rings, and polynomial rings",
        "statement": r"A subset containing \(1\) is a subring when it is closed under differences and products. For a set \(X\), functions \(X\to R\) form a ring pointwise, and \(R[x]\) is a ring under coefficientwise addition and convolution multiplication.",
        "proof": r"Closure under differences gives an additive subgroup, and product closure keeps multiplication inside the subset; associativity and distributivity are inherited. For functions, each ring axiom is checked at an arbitrary point \(x\in X\), reducing it to the corresponding axiom in \(R\). For polynomials, the coefficient of \(x^n\) in a product is \(\sum_{i+j=n}a_i b_j\); finite support makes every coefficient sum finite, and rearranging finite sums proves associativity and distributivity.",
        "example_title": "Polynomial multiplication as convolution",
        "example": r"For \(f=a_0+a_1x\) and \(g=b_0+b_1x\), one has \(fg=a_0b_0+(a_0b_1+a_1b_0)x+a_1b_1x^2\).",
    }],
    15: [{
        "title": "Characteristic and prime subrings",
        "statement": r"The characteristic of a ring \(R\) is the least positive \(n\) with \(n1_R=0\), or zero if no such \(n\) exists. The prime subring is isomorphic to \(\mathbb{Z}\) in characteristic zero and to \(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}\) in characteristic \(n\).",
        "proof": r"The map \(\eta:\mathbb{Z}\to R\), \(m\mapsto m1_R\), is a unital ring homomorphism. Its kernel is an ideal of \(\mathbb{Z}\), hence \(n\mathbb{Z}\) for a unique \(n\geq0\). The first isomorphism theorem identifies its image, the prime subring, with \(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}\). If \(R\) is a domain and \(n>0\), then \(n\) must be prime, since \(n=ab\) would give \((a1_R)(b1_R)=0\) with nonzero factors for a composite \(n\).",
        "example_title": "Characteristic of a matrix ring",
        "example": r"The ring \(M_2(\mathbb{F}_5)\) has characteristic five because \(5I=0\), while no smaller positive multiple of \(I\) vanishes.",
    }],
    18: [{
        "title": "Existence of maximal ideals",
        "statement": r"Every proper ideal in a nonzero commutative ring with identity is contained in a maximal ideal.",
        "proof": r"Order the proper ideals containing \(I\) by inclusion. A chain has an upper bound given by its union: the chain condition ensures closure under addition, and absorption is inherited. The union remains proper because if it contained \(1\), one member of the chain would already contain \(1\) and equal the whole ring. Zorn's lemma therefore supplies a maximal element \(M\). By construction, \(I\subseteq M\), and no proper ideal lies strictly above \(M\), so \(M\) is maximal.",
        "example_title": "Extending an ideal in a polynomial ring",
        "example": r"In \(\mathbb{R}[x,y]\), the proper ideal \((x)\) lies in \((x,y)\), and evaluation at \((0,0)\) shows \(\mathbb{R}[x,y]/(x,y)\cong\mathbb{R}\); hence \((x,y)\) is maximal.",
    }],
    19: [
        {
            "title": "Ascending chains and Noetherian control",
            "statement": r"A ring is Noetherian when every ascending chain of ideals stabilizes, equivalently when every ideal is finitely generated. Every PID is Noetherian.",
            "proof": r"If chains stabilize, build generators greedily: if \((a_1,\ldots,a_k)\neq I\), choose \(a_{k+1}\in I\) outside it. This would create a strictly increasing chain forever, so the process stops and \(I\) is finitely generated. Conversely, the union of an ascending chain is an ideal. If it is generated by finitely many elements, all generators occur in one member of the chain, after which the union and that member coincide. In a PID every ideal has one generator, so the condition holds immediately.",
            "example_title": "A nonstabilizing chain",
            "example": r"In \(F[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\), the chain \((x_1)\subsetneq(x_1,x_2)\subsetneq\cdots\) never stabilizes, so the infinitely generated polynomial ring is not Noetherian.",
        },
        {
            "title": "GCDs and Bezout identities in a PID",
            "statement": r"In a PID, \((a,b)=(d)\) where \(d\) is a greatest common divisor of \(a,b\), and \(d=ax+by\) for some \(x,y\in R\).",
            "proof": r"Since \((a,b)\) is principal, write it as \((d)\). Then \(a,b\in(d)\), so \(d\) divides both. Every common divisor \(c\) divides every linear combination of \(a,b\), and in particular divides \(d\), making \(d\) a gcd. Because \(d\in(a,b)\), the definition of the generated ideal gives \(d=ax+by\). Any two generators of the same principal ideal differ by a unit, which is exactly the usual ambiguity of a gcd.",
            "example_title": "Bezout in the integers",
            "example": r"The Euclidean calculation gives \(\gcd(30,18)=6=2\cdot30-3\cdot18\), so \((30,18)=(6)\).",
        },
    ],
    20: [{
        "title": "The Gaussian integers are Euclidean",
        "statement": r"The ring \(\mathbb{Z}[i]\) is Euclidean for the norm \(N(a+bi)=a^2+b^2\), and therefore is a PID and a UFD.",
        "proof": r"For \(\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{Z}[i]\) with \(\beta\neq0\), view \(\alpha/\beta\) in \(\mathbb{C}\). Choose a Gaussian integer \(q\) by rounding its real and imaginary parts to nearest integers, and set \(r=\alpha-\beta q\). The complex distance \(|\alpha/\beta-q|\) is at most \(\sqrt{1/2}<1\). Since the norm is multiplicative, \(N(r)=N(\beta)N(\alpha/\beta-q)<N(\beta)\). Thus \(\alpha=\beta q+r\) is Euclidean division.",
        "example_title": "One Gaussian division step",
        "example": r"For \(\alpha=7+5i\) and \(\beta=3+i\), \(\alpha/\beta=2.6+0.8i\). Choose \(q=3+i\); then \(r=-1-i\), and \(N(r)=2<N(\beta)=10\).",
    }],
    21: [
        {
            "title": "Gauss lemma and polynomial UFDs",
            "statement": r"If \(R\) is a UFD, a primitive polynomial in \(R[x]\) is reducible over the fraction field exactly when it is reducible in \(R[x]\). Consequently \(R[x]\) is a UFD.",
            "proof": r"The content \(c(f)\) is a gcd of the coefficients. Gauss lemma first proves \(c(fg)\sim c(f)c(g)\): reducing modulo any prime shows that a prime dividing every coefficient of \(fg\) must divide all coefficients of one factor. If a primitive \(f\) factors in the fraction field, clear denominators to obtain \(df=gh\) in \(R[x]\). Separate contents from primitive parts and use content multiplicativity; cancellation of the scalar contents leaves a factorization of \(f\) by positive-degree polynomials in \(R[x]\). Combining irreducible scalar factors with primitive polynomial factors gives unique factorization.",
            "example_title": "Moving between coefficient fields",
            "example": r"The primitive polynomial \(x^2-2\) is irreducible in \(\mathbb{Z}[x]\) because it is irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}\); a rational factorization would clear to an integer one.",
        },
        {
            "title": "Eisenstein's irreducibility criterion",
            "statement": r"If a prime \(p\) divides every nonleading coefficient of \(f\in\mathbb{Z}[x]\), does not divide the leading coefficient, and \(p^2\) does not divide the constant term, then \(f\) is irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}\).",
            "proof": r"By Gauss lemma, a rational factorization would give a factorization by primitive integer polynomials. Reduce such a factorization modulo \(p\). The hypotheses make \(\overline f\) a nonzero monomial, so both reduced factors are monomials. Their constant terms are therefore divisible by \(p\). The product constant term would then be divisible by \(p^2\), contradicting the final hypothesis. Hence no nontrivial factorization exists.",
            "example_title": "An Eisenstein polynomial",
            "example": r"The polynomial \(x^5+10x+5\) is Eisenstein at \(p=5\): five divides both nonleading coefficients, twenty-five does not divide the constant term, and five does not divide the leading coefficient.",
        },
    ],
})


MIT_18_703_LECTURE_EXTENSIONS.update({
    22: [{
        "title": "Finite multiplicative subgroups are cyclic",
        "statement": r"Every finite subgroup \(G\) of the multiplicative group of a field is cyclic. In particular, \(\mathbb{F}_q^\times\) is cyclic of order \(q-1\).",
        "proof": r"Let \(m\) be the exponent of \(G\), the least common multiple of all element orders. Every element is a root of \(X^m-1\), so the field root bound gives \(|G|\leq m\). On the other hand, every element order divides \(|G|\), hence \(m\mid|G|\) and \(m\leq|G|\). Thus \(m=|G|\). For each prime-power divisor of \(m\), choose an element whose order contains that full prime power; taking the product of the corresponding commuting powers gives an element of order \(m\). That element generates \(G\).",
        "example_title": "A generator of a finite field's units",
        "example": r"In \(\mathbb{F}_7^\times\), the element \(3\) has successive powers \(3,2,6,4,5,1\), so it generates all six nonzero classes.",
    }],
    23: [
        {
            "title": "Cauchy's theorem from a group action",
            "statement": r"If a prime \(p\) divides \(|G|\), then \(G\) contains an element of order \(p\).",
            "proof": r"Let \(X=\{(g_1,\ldots,g_p)\in G^p:g_1\cdots g_p=e\}\). Choosing the first \(p-1\) entries freely determines the last, so \(|X|=|G|^{p-1}\), divisible by \(p\). The cyclic group \(C_p\) acts on \(X\) by rotating coordinates; rotation preserves membership because \(g_1\cdots g_p=e\) implies \(g_2\cdots g_pg_1=e\). Nonfixed orbits have size \(p\), so the number of fixed points is divisible by \(p\). Fixed points are \((g,\ldots,g)\) with \(g^p=e\). Besides \(g=e\), there must be another, and its order is \(p\).",
            "example_title": "An element of order three",
            "example": r"Because \(3\mid|A_4|=12\), Cauchy's theorem guarantees an element of order three; the three-cycles provide the concrete elements.",
        },
        {
            "title": "Automorphisms of symmetric groups",
            "statement": r"For \(n\neq6\), every automorphism of \(S_n\) is inner. For \(n=6\), the outer automorphism group has order two.",
            "proof": r"An automorphism preserves element orders and conjugacy-class sizes. For \(n\neq6\), these invariants characterize the class of transpositions, so every automorphism sends transpositions to transpositions. The incidence relation between transpositions records which two-element subsets share a label; preserving it produces a permutation \(\tau\) of the labels. The automorphism then agrees with conjugation by \(\tau\) on all transpositions, which generate \(S_n\), so it is inner. In \(S_6\), transpositions share their order and class size with a second cycle type, allowing the exceptional outer symmetry.",
            "example_title": "Inner relabeling",
            "example": r"Conjugation by \(\tau\) sends \((i\,j)\) to \((\tau(i)\,\tau(j))\); since transpositions generate \(S_n\), these images determine the automorphism.",
        },
    ],
})


